NTPD(1)                      User Commands

NAME
    ntpd - clock synchronisation over SNTP

SYNOPSIS
    ntpd [-1] [-c FILE]
    ntpd -q

DESCRIPTION
    ntpd sets the clock from time servers with the Simple Network Time
    Protocol, version 4 (RFC 4330). init starts it as the service ntp.
    The servers come from /etc/ntp.conf, one per line as "server NAME"
    or "server NAME port N", and are tried in order. Without a server
    line ntpd asks pool.ntp.org.

    A query sends one request and computes the offset of the local
    clock and the round trip delay from the four time stamps of the
    exchange. An answer is refused when it is shorter than 48 bytes,
    has another mode than server, another version than 3 or 4, a server
    that reports no synchronization, a stratum outside 1 to 15, an
    originate time that is not the time of the request, or a transmit
    time of zero. An offset above 128 ms steps the clock with
    clock_settime(2). A smaller offset is corrected gradually with
    adjtime(2), at 500 microseconds per second.

    The service queries every 15 minutes, and every minute after a
    failure. It writes each result to the system log and the state of
    the last query to /run/ntpd.state.

OPTIONS
    -1        query once, correct the clock, print the result and exit
              with status 0, or 1 when no server answered
    -c FILE   read the servers from FILE
    -q        print the state of the last query

FILES
    /etc/ntp.conf, /run/ntpd.state, /var/log/messages

SEE ALSO
    date(1), init.conf(5)
